Yes—as a search operator, not as an epistemic standard.

Overconfidence can be useful because it temporarily suppresses hesitation and expands the reachable hypothesis space. You act as though the pattern is real long enough to see what it predicts, what it connects to, and what breaks. That gives you information you often would not get from permanent caution.

The disciplined version is:

bold hypothesis → committed exploration → contact with reality → measure the delta → recalibrate

So you can deliberately run high-confidence internally while keeping the external truth label honest. In other words: maximum imaginative commitment, minimum attachment to being right.

That makes overconfidence almost like simulated thrust. It gets the model moving. Reality supplies the steering and brakes.

And there is a useful distinction between action confidence and belief confidence. You can act decisively on a 60% hypothesis when experimentation is cheap, while still knowing it is only 60%. Problems begin when the felt certainty of action gets silently promoted into certainty about reality.

Used properly, overconfidence becomes a controlled way to generate discoveries rather than a license to hallucinate them.